From fba69dd8a437812aa92c6bc11aec83d8a1bcdd0f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 07:58:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: bug 35: cache the compiled polymorphic-FFI
 textures

`compilePolymorphicFFI` (lib/Conversion/CompilePolymorphicFFI) compiles
every texture instance with its own rustc run
(`compileRustSourceToBitcode`, lib/RustCompiler), one after another, and
programs built against the same packages compile the same textures again
on every build. With large packages the runs take most of a build: a
small lean2rr program has 474 textures, and rrc spends 16 s on it, all
but about 2 s in rustc.

When REUSSIR_FFI_CACHE_DIR names a directory, keep the bitcode rustc
produces there, one file per key, and read it back instead of running
rustc. The key is a BLAKE3 digest of the source, rustc's options (all but
the temporary source and output files), rustc's resolved path, content
and `-vV` report (a wrapper script and the toolchain a rustup proxy runs
are told apart), the host CPU and its features (`-C target-cpu=native`),
and the content of every .rlib, .rmeta and shared library in the package
directories (an `-L` kind such as `crate=DIR` stripped).

That toolchain part is computed once per process. Every file it read is
stamped (device, inode, size, modification time and, on POSIX systems,
status-change time) and checked again before each lookup and each store,
so a library replaced while rrc runs, by rename or in place, gets a new
digest, and a texture compiled meanwhile is not stored. A package
directory's own stamp changes with any entry added or removed (rrc's
temporary files when it runs there, a cache directory made there); a
changed directory stamp counts only if the libraries listed in it
changed, and is refreshed otherwise. When something cannot be read or
run, caching is off for the run, with one warning on stderr.

An entry is the digest of the bitcode followed by the bitcode, written to
a temporary file in the directory (named by process and a random number
in the file name only) and renamed into place; an entry that cannot be
read, does not match its digest or does not start with a bitcode magic is
a miss. Without the variable nothing changes, except that rustc gets the
source and `-o OUT` first on its command line (the options keep their
relative order).

Tests: frontend/polyffi_cache.rr (a second build takes its textures from
the cache with compilation disabled; other options and a truncated entry
are misses) and frontend/polyffi_cache_swap.rr (a library replaced during
the first texture compile, by rename or in place with its size and
modification time kept, then restored: the next build sees the restored
library; a `crate=DIR` package directory is hashed; rrc running in a
package directory stores its entries).
